                "node": {
                    "created_at": "1775813961",
                    "text": "In the realm of digital chaos, few things are as terrifyingly impressive as the \"zip bomb.\" This viral Discord screenshot features a user who has allegedly created a file named funny.zip that decompresses to a staggering **55.4 Yottabytes**. To put that into perspective, the post notes that as of 2016, total global internet traffic was only about **1.1 Zettabytes** per year. This tiny 42 KB file contains enough recursive data to effectively \"explode\" any hardware that attempts to unextract it, filling up storage space until the system crashes.\nWhile it’s often used as a legendary internet prank, the zip bomb is a masterclass in data compression and a stark reminder that you should never trust a random download—no matter how \"funny\" it claims to be. In 2026, where our lives are entirely cloud-based, a 55-yottabyte file is basically a digital black hole. Download with caution, or your hard drive might just retire early!\n#ZipBomb #CyberSecurity #TechMemes #DigitalChaos #ProgrammingHumor",
                    "id": "17853302139646383"
                }
            }
        ]
    },
